WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.502
In most estates, the certificate
authority belongs to whoever installed it,

2
00:00:04.502 --> 00:00:07.612
and by default the domain's
top administrators run it.

3
00:00:07.612 --> 00:00:09.087
Nobody decided that.

4
00:00:09.087 --> 00:00:13.157
In the target, the estate spreads
across more systems, not fewer,

5
00:00:13.157 --> 00:00:17.538
each with administrators holding
a piece of what the agency trusts.

6
00:00:17.538 --> 00:00:20.713
So the program separates who
decides from who does the work.

7
00:00:20.713 --> 00:00:25.374
The CISO sets issuance policy,
trust anchors and binding strength.

8
00:00:25.374 --> 00:00:28.900
The authorizing official accepts
every standing exception.

9
00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:33.962
The sponsor and CIO decide the hierarchy
and when old authorities retire.

10
00:00:33.962 --> 00:00:38.729
And their decision here: name one renewal
owner for every certificate class,

11
00:00:38.729 --> 00:00:40.999
recorded in the lifecycle platform.

12
00:00:40.999 --> 00:00:45.140
Because an expiry is almost always
an ownership failure first.
